Before running the following code, please ensure that the following dependencies are installed in your local Python environment: xgboost, pandas, numpy, matplotlib, scikit-learn, scikit-optimize (skopt), shap, scikit-fuzzy (skfuzzy), networkx, and mlxtend.
Using the MTUH dataset as an example, the following demonstrates the code execution process for the rule mining technique based on interpretable ensemble learning. The specific steps to run the code are as follows:
Step 1: Place the MTUH training and test set data in the same directory as the code, and prepare the data files “trainH.csv” and “testH.csv”. Run the source code “01_hyperparameter_tuning.py”. To begin, uncomment the grid search section to perform coarse tuning, and then run the Bayesian search section (BayesSearchCV) to perform fine-tuning. After execution, the optimal hyperparameter combination is output for use in the next phase of model building.
Step 2: Based on the optimal hyperparameters for BO-XGBoost determined in Step 1, modify the model parameters (such as max_depth, learning_rate, n_estimators, etc.) in the source code file “02_shap_feature_explanation.py”. After execution, the global interpretation graph and the feature interaction graph are output in sequence. By modifying the feat_x and feat_inter variables, the collaborative interactions among different feature groups can be examined.
Step 3: Based on the attribute-reduced key feature data from MTUH, run the source code “03_fcm_clustering.py.” Set the number of cluster centers to n_clusters = 3, and output the cluster center matrix.
Step 4: Enter the clustering center points for each attribute (high, medium, low) into the main_func parameter in the source code file “04_membership_boolean_encoding.py” (i.e., as the inflection points for the triangular membership function). After execution, specific fine-grained numerical values can be mapped to values in a fuzzy semantic space and expanded into Boolean data using 0 and 1 encoding.
Step 5: Based on the fuzzy data table, run the source code “05_fuzzy_similarity_matrix.py” to generate a fuzzy similarity matrix using Euclidean distance.
Step 6: Input the fuzzy similarity matrix, run the source code “06_mst_data_compression.py,” construct a maximum spanning tree using the Kruskal algorithm and visualize it, and output a weighted network topology diagram named maximum_spanning_tree.png. Set a truncation threshold based on edge weights, remove weakly connected edges to obtain connected components, and eliminate singular classes to compress the sample space data.
Step 7: Based on the Boolean database derived from the compressed MTUH sample set, set the thresholds for the parameters SUPP, CONF, and MAXLEN. Run the source code “07_association_rule_mining.py” to extract strong association rules that meet the confidence and support thresholds, and build the rule knowledge base.
